WEBVTT
TITLE IP Support Protocols: ARP, DHCP and ICMP — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
An IP packet names hosts. The frame that carries it names interfaces.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Three helpers sit beside IPv4: ARP finds a MAC, DHCP finds an IP, ICMP reports trouble.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Each one is identified differently: an EtherType, a protocol number, or a pair of ports.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Without them the first packet never leaves the host.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A wants to send to B and knows only B’s IP address. Its ARP cache is empty.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The request goes to the broadcast MAC address, so every host on the wire reads it.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Only the owner of that address answers, and the answer is unicast to the asker.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The pair is cached, and 28 octets of ARP are padded to a 64-octet frame.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Compare the destination with your own prefix. Inside, ask for the destination’s MAC.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Outside, ask for the router’s MAC. The IP destination still names the far host.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Five destinations, one /24 prefix: four inside, one outside.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
So four requests name a host and one names the gateway.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
A gratuitous ARP reply announces your own mapping without being asked.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
A DHCP client sends one after its lease, so the subnet learns the new pair.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Proxy ARP lets a router answer for an address that is not on the wire.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
An unsolicited reply can also poison a cache. IPv6 replaced ARP with discovery.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A new host has no address, so its source is 0.0.0.0 and its destination is 255.255.255.255.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Discover is broadcast from port 68 to port 67. Every server on the segment may offer.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The client requests one offer, still by broadcast, so the other servers learn the choice.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The server acknowledges. Four messages, and the client now has an address.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A lease is a timer. T1 is half of it and T2 is seven eighths of it.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
At T1 the client asks its own server again, and that request is unicast.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
If nothing arrives by T2, the client asks any server, and that one is broadcast.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
A relay agent carries the message across a router, and 236 fixed octets hold the fields.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
ICMP travels inside IP with protocol number 1. It has no ports and carries no data.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Five types are errors and the rest are queries. The header is 8 octets.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
An error quotes the IP header and the first 8 octets of the datagram that caused it.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
ICMP reports a problem. It never repairs one, and IP stays unreliable.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
No error about an ICMP error, or about a broadcast or multicast destination.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
No error about a link-layer broadcast, or about any fragment except the first.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
No error when the source is not one single host.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Five rules, six cases: four messages and two silences.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
ping sends an echo request, type 8, and reads the echo reply, type 0.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Traceroute sends with TTL 1, then 2, then 3. Each router that drops a packet reports it.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Each report is type 11 code 0. On a five-hop path that is four reports.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The fifth packet reaches the host, which answers port unreachable: type 3, code 3.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Ask what is missing: a hardware address, an IP address, or a report about a packet.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
A missing MAC is ARP, a missing IP is DHCP, and trouble is ICMP. Then check the prefix.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Count broadcasts, not messages: one ARP request, and two DHCP broadcasts per lease.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Name the layer and the identifier, and never call ICMP a transport protocol.

